          {
            "source_quote": "τινὶ γὰρ προφητείαν ἐπαγγέλλεσθαι θέλοντι ἐὰν συγχωρηθῇ πιστεύεσθαι ἐν οἷς ψεύστης φωρᾶται ὅτι τότε τὸ τῆς προγνώσεως ἅγιον οὐκ εἶχεν πνεῦμα, οὐκ εὐέλεγκτος ἔσται ψεύστης ὑπάρχων προφήτης·",
            "english_quote": "For if someone wishing to profess prophecy is allowed to be believed, in the matters in which he is caught as a liar, that at that time he did not have the holy spirit of foreknowledge, he will not be easy to expose, though he is a prophet who is a liar.",
            "issue": "The Greek presents the absence of the spirit as the explanation permitted to be believed when the claimant is exposed as a liar. The English construction “someone ... is allowed to be believed ... that” does not grammatically attach the content clause to that belief. The intended meaning is recoverable, but the Greek construction needs an intelligible English equivalent.",
            "suggested_revision": "Replace “is allowed to be believed” with “is allowed to have it believed,” retaining the following qualification and “that” clause. This proposed correction requires fresh review; it does not require changing or verifying the supplied Greek.",
